<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
  <title>Cognition in Adolescence</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00053</md:content-id>
    <md:uuid>3c7b88f8-7985-454e-bc14-9f1b249c2528</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001">
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <para id="para-00001">By the end of this section, you will be able to:</para>
    <list id="list-00001">
    <item>Describe Piaget’s theory of the formal operational stage of cognitive development in adolescence</item>
    <item>Describe the information processing perspective on cognitive development in adolescence</item>
    <item>Explain influences that promote or inhibit cognitive development in adolescence</item>
    </list>
    </section>
    <para id="para-00002">Anna is a 17-year-old student in twelfth grade in a public school. She recently sought counselling because she was having trouble dealing with the stress of her senior year. Coming from a culture that places great importance on academic success, Anna faces intense pressure from her parents to achieve high grades and attend university. She does not want to let them down but is constantly feeling exhausted and anxious. She overthinks everything she does, and after every interaction she wonders how people might have perceived her or thought of her behavior, which gets exhausting and stressful. This has led her to not socialize with people, and she tries to avoid interactions with others as much as possible. What can Anna do to overcome such thoughts and deal with academic stress?</para>
    <para id="para-00003">You know that adolescence is a time of transitions. You’ve explored the development of brain structures, and now you’ll consider the functional consequences of those profound physical changes. In one sense, if we consider the brain our “hardware,” we might think of the mind as our “software,” or perhaps a collection of apps with an operating system. In this section, you’ll turn our attention to the developing capabilities of the adolescent’s new “operating system.” In other words, what may be going on in Anna’s brain functioning that is triggering anxiety around exams, and self-judgment when it comes to peer interactions?</para>
    <section id="sect-00002">
    <title>Piaget’s Theory: The Formal Operations Stage</title>
    <para id="para-00004">In considering the cognitive development of the adolescent, we’ve reached what Piaget considered the final universal stage, labeled <term id="term-00001">formal operations</term>. We use the word <emphasis effect="italics">formal</emphasis> to signify that the adolescent has achieved the capability to reason using formal logical systems and symbols. In a larger sense, Piaget considered cognitive development complete during adolescence – the adolescent has achieved formal adult thinking capabilities. Here you’ll consider the advances adolescents make and take a critical look at Piaget’s theory to see where (research has shown it requires some revision) it may require some revision.</para>
    <para id="para-00005">We can connect the key developments in adolescent cognition to the single theme of <emphasis effect="italics">possibilities</emphasis>. Adolescents can think about all the possibilities in a situation beforehand and then test them systematically (Crain, 2005). School-age children can think in rather complex and logical ways, but they are limited to reasoning about concrete, physical situations in the here and now. Adolescents, in addition to increasing the complexity of their reasoning, gain the ability to think about all aspects of a situation—even a situation that doesn’t (or even can’t) physically exist. For example, high school history teachers might ask their adolescent students to imagine they could go back in time to change just one event and write about the consequences. A school-aged child would struggle with this assignment.</para>
    <para id="para-00006">Piaget discovered the major changes in cognitive abilities using the clinical method we’ve discussed earlier. Through a series of tests, demonstrations, clinical interviews, and careful observation, he outlined the advanced cognitive capabilities of adolescents. You’ll learn about each skill in turn, but they all come back to two big achievements: thinking about what is possible, and adopting the systematic use of formal logic and reason.</para>
    <section id="sect-00003">
    <title>Hypothetical-Deductive Reasoning</title>
    <para id="para-00007">We derive many cognitive skills and developments from our ability to think about what might be possible. <term id="term-00002">Hypothetical reasoning</term>, for example, lets us predict what might occur given a set of circumstances: If a storm cloud is present and the air feels humid, it may rain soon. <term id="term-00003">Deductive reasoning</term> helps us test statements, called propositions, in a logical and orderly way. Not only do adolescents gain the ability think about all the possibilities and angles of a particular problem, but they achieve the mindset to systematically explore and test each one.</para>
    <para id="para-00008">Piaget devised several now-classic scenarios to test aspects of his theory. His pendulum problem is perhaps the most famous test of deductive reasoning and a hallmark of high school science classes around the Western world. The problem asks which of four variables makes the pendulum swing faster—the length of the string, the height from which the weight on the string is dropped, the amount of weight, or the force of the initial push? What’s important is not what the right answer is, but how the participant goes about discovering it. While a child in the concrete operational stage (7 to 11 years old) might try out several different solutions seemingly at random and quickly come up with an answer, correct or not, the adolescent will methodically plan and test many possible combinations of the four variables, having thought ahead about to do so and how the four might interact with one another (<link target-id="fig-00001"/>).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00001">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_03_Pendulum.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>The pendulum task is used to identify a formal operational thinker, who is able to think logically even when dealing with abstract concepts or hypothetical situations.</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00009">This systematic testing of possibilities is the hallmark of <term id="term-00004">scientific thinking </term>and relies on the adolescent’s newfound ability to reason using <term id="term-00005">propositional logic</term>. Taken together, these cognitive capabilities allow adolescents to discover complex relations and truths about the world.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
    <title>Multidimensional Facets of Adolescent Thinking</title>
    <para id="para-00010">Adolescent thought is characterized by several other tendencies that stem from teens’ new ability to think about what is possible. First is the ability to think in complex ways about abstract ideas. Adolescents become aware of the world of ideas that don’t necessarily exist in physical form, and concepts like justice, equality, equity, and truth take on new and profound importance. A child in the concrete operations stage might be able to talk about equality in simple terms, focusing on a physical example of the concept. An adolescent’s description would be much more nuanced and might include a discussion of equality of opportunity and equal rights (another abstract yet real concept).</para>
    <para id="para-00011">Adolescents are also able to think about multiple dimensions of a problem or situation, and the way various factors combine to influence an observed outcome. For example, when faced with a dress code violation at school, a high school student might be able to see how the policy creates a standard against which all students are held, while also arguing against the lost class time that being sent home to change entails. Seeing the irony of such a situation is a hallmark of adolescent thinking and may lead to frustration and de-idealization of the adults who control their world. An appreciation for sarcasm, which relies on the ability to think in multiple dimensions, also emerges in adolescence.</para>
    <para id="para-00012">Because they can now think about the possible, in abstract ways and in multiple dimensions, adolescents also develop <term id="term-00006">relativistic thinking</term>, the belief that most truths and statements of fact are relative to the position of the observer. When all things are possible and all perspectives can be considered, everyone can seem correct – and everyone can seem wrong. It’s all a matter of perspective. Questioning rules, especially rules that seem arbitrary, is another hallmark of the adolescent experience. We’ll explore more of the social consequences of perspective-taking in <link document="m00056"/>.</para>
    <note class="intersections-contexts" id="note-00001">
    <title>Adolescent Idealism and Changing the World</title>
    <para id="para-00013">Where does idealism come from? A quick review of Piaget’s theory of cognitive development provides several relevant themes that combine to give adolescence this distinct characteristic. The major new ability to think about possibilities is the root answer to why idealism emerges in adolescence. Along with that comes the ability to think logically about complex abstract concepts, even going so far as to test out hypotheses in one’s head. Adolescent theorist David Elkind was pivotal in exploring the consequences of these combined abilities (Elkind, 1998; Tyler, 2020). He noted that as adolescents grow in their analytical capabilities, it’s natural for them to become critical of the world around them. Suddenly, they notice their parents driving over the speed limit, a teacher with alcohol on his breath, or a police officer on her cell phone while driving. And turning on the TV or scanning social media shows a world rife with hunger, war, homelessness, and political unrest.</para>
    <para id="para-00014">Adolescents react to this perception of disorder and hypocrisy in a predictable way, according to Elkind. Remember that adolescents are growing into adulthood, and the desire to take on adult responsibilities and roles adds to their determination to see the wrongs in the world and to do better! A search for solutions becomes personal and emotionally charged. The not-quite-adult individual has the capacity to imagine a better world and wants to take immediate action. Teachers, healthcare professionals, youth religious workers, and other adult mentors are well aware of the constructive power of idealism. Joining political clubs at school, getting involved in community projects, and arguing with adults are all part of this newfound sense of what’s possible and what can be made right. Rather than seeing adolescents’ argumentativeness and political cries as “acting out,” adults can recognize their idealism—and the accompanying de-idealization of parents and other authority figures—as a normative part of the transition to adulthood, and can work with adolescents to help them channel their interests and energy.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00005">
    <title>Adolescent Egocentrism</title>
    <para id="para-00015"><term id="term-00007">Adolescent egocentrism</term> is the tendency for an adolescent to believe other people, sometimes called the <term id="term-00008">imaginary audience</term>, are as obsessed with the teen’s appearance, body, and behavior as they are (Elkind, 1967, p.1030). Teens may feel that everyone is watching them and have a strong need to impress others, though usually their peers are also consumed by what others think of them and don’t necessarily worry about what <emphasis effect="italics">they</emphasis> think about others. This recognition might help someone like Anna in this section’s opening story if she got some advice from parents and counselors.</para>
    <para id="para-00016">According to Elkind, another aspect of adolescent egocentrism is the <term id="term-00009">personal fable</term>. That is, teenagers often believe they and their feelings are unique, and that no one else can feel suffering with such intensity (Elkind, 1967, p.1031). Obsession with the personal fable and concerns about pleasing the imaginary audience likely peak around age fifteen, along with self-consciousness in general.</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00002">
    <para id="para-00017">Why do teenagers often appear more impulsive and less self-aware compared to adults? Cognitive neuroscientist <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104TEDBlakemore">Sarah-Jayne Blakemore examines the differences between the prefrontal cortex of adolescents and that of adults</link> to illustrate how typically “teenage” behaviors stem from the growing and developing brain.</para>
    </note>
    <para id="para-00018">Social media can reinforce teens’ belief that they are unique (Houlihan, 2014). Online bullying is an apt example, given that adolescents feel they are invulnerable to the negative outcomes of their actions (Houlihan, 2014). Being bullied damages the ego, however, and can lead to emotional struggles worsened by teen victims’ still-developing cognitive maturity.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00006">
    <title>Evaluating Piaget’s Theory</title>
    <para id="para-00019">Piaget’s theory of cognitive development was a revolution in the scientific study of the child. It was the first theory to offer a complete accounting of the cognitive skills that exist in children of various ages, as well as the first to give a comprehensive explanation of the way those skills develop in the early decades of the lifespan. Piaget is also lauded for applying the techniques of the clinical interview method to the study of the child. His theory is not without flaws, however. We’ll discuss several of these in turn.</para>
    <para id="para-00020">First, recall that Piaget’s theory of cognitive development is a stage theory. This means that children learn specific cognitive skills at different ages that map directly to their brain maturation. It turns out, however, that development might not occur exactly in the stepwise fashion implied by the term “stage theory.” Rather, cognitive abilities and mature thinking can occur in a continuous fashion, given the proper guidance from others. Basically, Piaget’s focus on the role of nature and the brain’s physical maturation led to him to underestimate the significant roles that culture and social interaction and experience play in cognitive development (Franzoi, 2011; Lourenço, 2016).</para>
    <para id="para-00021">Lev Vygotsky suggested that children’s cognitive development occurs within a sociocultural context, rather than through interactions with physical objects as Jean Piaget suggested. Vygotsky noted that cultural tools like language and counting systems have a significant influence on cognitive growth (Bernstein et al., 2008).</para>
    <para id="para-00022">Second, the interview style Piaget used often relied heavily on children’s verbal descriptions of their thought processes (Ginsburg &amp; Opper, 1988; Müller et al., 2009). This works well for children who have a well-developed vocabulary and insight into their own thinking. A child who lacks these skills, however, may not be able to explain their actual understanding, and the Piagetian approach may mistakenly claim the child doesn’t understand the concept in question. Piaget’s technique was also prone to focus on what children of various ages were incapable of doing or simply got wrong. His tendency to equate task performance with competency has been criticized (Lourenço, 2016).</para>
    <para id="para-00023">One final criticism is of Piaget’s view of formal operations and scientific thinking as the end-state for human cognitive development. This bias likely reflects the high value Western societies place on this style of thinking. Other societies and cultures have other perspectives on the ultimate goals of cognition, however, including a focus on artistic expression, wisdom represented in folklore, or practical success in everyday life (Lourenço, 2016; Martin et al., 2010).	In some cultures, educational institutions don’t emphasize critical thinking, which is needed to reach a formal operational stage. As a result, only about half of the people in these societies achieve this level of thinking skills (Bernstein et al., 2008). Additionally, some biological psychologists suggest that teenagers worldwide cannot handle complex calculations effectively until the age of 20 because their brain activity is still developing (Cacioppo &amp; Freyberg, 2013).</para>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00007">
    <title>Information Processing Perspective</title>
    <para id="para-00024">The information processing perspective on cognitive development retains several features of Piaget’s theory while adding a more contemporary focus on the biological supports for various thinking abilities or <term id="term-00010">cognitive domains</term>. These domains include processing and perceptual speed, attention, memory, spatial reasoning, verbal ability, numerical ability, and metacognition (<link target-id="fig-00002"/>).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00002">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_03_Changes.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Cognitive Information Processing Model (IPM) that illustrates the role of rehearsal in input, storage, and retrieval of information.</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00025">The brain growth and maturation we’ve already discussed lead to rapid increases in abilities across all the cognitive domains. Most of these increases continue into early adulthood before leveling off. One notable exception is <term id="term-00011">perceptual speed</term>, the ability to quickly recognize objects and other symbols, which peaks in late adolescence and then declines throughout the rest of life (Kail &amp; Ferrer, 2007; Schaie, 1994).</para>
    <para id="para-00026"><term id="term-00012">Metacognition</term>, the ability to think about thinking, is another rapidly developing feature of adolescent thought and a critical skill for complex problem solving. Using metacognition, we can take a moment to pause and form a plan for approaching a problem, selectively choose the strategy that might work, and monitor our progress toward a solution. We can also decide how much to rely on our own decisions or on the advice of others (Koriat, 2007). The adolescent’s growing awareness and regulation of their own cognitive abilities makes for a formidable and nearly mature thinker who begins to rely less on others’ input.</para>
    <para id="para-00027">Do adolescents reliably use these capabilities in everyday life? A recent study noted that teens aspire to autonomy in decision-making and do harness their emerging abilities to reject misleading advice but accept helpful guidance from others (Moses-Payne et al., 2021). Further, a significant proportion of teen behavior reflects some reasoning. Reasoned risk-taking involves careful planning and strategy, depending on improved cognitive control and a strong inclination for seeking new experiences (Romer et al., 2017). A recent study noted that risky behavior was linked not only to higher levels of sensation-seeking behaviors but also to better working memory and a stronger focus on the future (Maslowsky et al., 2019).</para>
    <para id="para-00028">Thus, as we consider the adolescent from the information processing perspective, we encounter individuals who are increasingly advanced across many cognitive domains, together with an ever more sophisticated ability to reason effectively using those skills. By and large, by the end of adolescence, a highly capable individual with considerable cognitive prowess has emerged.</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00003">
    <para id="para-00029">In this <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104TEDGalvan">TED talk, Adriana Galván reveals the neurobiology behind typical teenage behaviors</link> like risk-taking, exploration and thrill-seeking. Watch the video to gain insights into the opportunities and vulnerabilities associated with the teen brain and behavior development, which can help shape policies, juvenile justice and public health concerns that impact young people.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00008">
    <title>Influences on Cognitive Development</title>
    <para id="para-00030">Both Piaget’s theory and the information processing perspective are <term id="term-00013">interactionist</term> in nature. In cognitive development, this means that development is fueled by a feedback loop between the individual’s maturing brain and the environmental inputs and supports for the brain’s further growth and development. A good example is the U.S. school curricula at various grade levels. The preschool curriculum seems ideally suited to take advantage of the fantastical and playful mindset of the preoperational child, while high school curricula leverage adolescents’ newfound abilities and appreciation for complex problem solving, introducing trigonometry and abstract concepts, among other challenges. Because of this back-and-forth “conversation” between the individual and the environment, we should expect to find several major contexts that create fertile ground for cognitive development.</para>
    <para id="para-00031">The first of these is the family and home environment (<link target-id="fig-00003"/>). Once again, the authoritative parenting style (recall Baumrind from earlier chapters) shows the most benefit for school performance and engagement (Steinberg et al., 1992) if we consider the development of formal operations to be the end goal of cognitive development. Authoritative parents set high expectations, but they also explain the reasoning behind rules and encourage their children to extract general principles from experiences and explore within boundaries. These parent–child interactions reinforce the reasoning fostered at school. Another way the family and home environment support cognitive development is by providing learning resources and opportunities for practice and exploration. The types of games, reading materials, preferred TV shows, and family vacations all offer opportunities to learn about the world and practice that understanding (Greenfield, 2014; Wu et al., 2021). There are clear socioeconomic differences in the amount and types of learning-relevant resources available in homes.</para>
    <para id="para-00032">Second, schooling is correlated has a documented close relationship with advancing cognition. For example, during the later high school grades in the United States, many students take a civics class, where they not only learn about the government and its functions and procedures but also tackle thorny civic issues facing society, often with a variety of moral and legal implications. Topics like regulating guns or the internet are perfectly relevant for adolescents to develop and practice their new cognitive toolkit. Student government and other schoolwide organizations also give students a chance to try putting their ideas about the possible into practice in their everyday lives.</para>
    <figure id="fig-00003">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_03_CogDevFact.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00033">Finally, active influences on cognitive development operate at the cultural level. In cultures that don’t emphasize the development of scientific reasoning, Piaget’s stage of formal operations may not be reached, and the features associated with adolescent thought might not be readily observed (Cole, 2006; Nur et al., 2020).</para>
    <para id="para-00034">A growing body of scientific literature shows that cognition and cognitive development are at least partially dependent upon cultural input (Kitayama &amp; Uskul, 2011). Regarding attention and memory, for example, psychologists have found that people growing up in Japan pay more attention to objects in a holistic (big-picture and relational) way, whereas people growing up in the United States tend to pinpoint the main focal points of a scene. On a memory task, Japanese participants were able to recall more items from the background of a complex image than U.S. participants (Masuda &amp; Nisbett, 2001). A similar result was found by Wong and colleagues (2018) who tested thirty-six (36) Caucasian Canadians in Toronto and 36 Han Chinese in Beijing to investigate cultural differences for individual objects and backgrounds using a picture recognition paradigm. Results noted that Canadian participants demonstrated notably superior memory for backgrounds compared to Chinese participants. , and that the Chinese participants may engage more in holistic processing, which complicates their ability to isolate background information from associated focal objects, unlike Canadian counterparts (Wong et al., 2018).</para>
    <para id="para-00035">Through the structure of language, culture can support and propel learning and cognitive development. For example, in Chinese languages, fractions are represented simply as parts of a whole, so ¼ is said as “one out of four” instead of “one-fourth” (Xin &amp; Liu, 2015). This simplicity and uniformity make learning and manipulating fractions easier and may contribute to observed cross-cultural differences in mathematics performance.</para>
    <para id="para-00036">To the extent that a culture (or even an individual home) emphasizes the manipulation of visual symbols such as icons, emojis, and other visual symbol systems, it is providing everyday practice for tests of fluid intelligence. Think for a moment how visual our current world is—we’re constantly tapping on apps and icons and manipulating symbols on screens, often with different fonts and abbreviations. This highly visual, nonverbal environment supports the development of our spatial and perceptual processing skills (Williams, 1998; Blazhenkova &amp; Kozhevnikov, 2010). Children and adolescents from cultures that do not possess these types of environments or do not emphasize them will be at a disadvantage on tests of such skills.</para>
    </section>
    <section class="summary" id="sect-00009">
    <list id="list-00002">
    <item>Teen cognition as explained by Piaget's cognitive development theory suggests that adolescents undergo significant cognitive changes, including the ability to think abstractly and engage in hypothetical reasoning. The information processing model emphasizes how adolescents acquire, store, and retrieve information, highlighting improvements in attention, memory, and problem-solving skills.</item>
    <item>Adolescent Egocentrism describes teens’ tendency to focus on their own thoughts and feelings while struggling to consider perspectives different from their own, a phenomenon often observed during this developmental stage.</item>
    <item>Adolescent cognitive development is influenced not only by biological factors such as brain maturation and hormonal changes, but also environmental factors including social interactions, access to education, peer motivation, and parental expectations. These influences shape teenagers' thinking abilities, decision-making skills, and overall cognitive development.</item>
    </list>
    </section>
